---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Divider from '../components/Divider.astro';
---

<Base
  title="The hunt — Call Me Maybe"
  description="A scavenger hunt that walks every feature of the house phone, played by a child with a cordless handset and won by paging the whole house. The prize is extra rice."
>
  <header class="lobby" style="padding-block:clamp(2.5rem,6vw,4rem) 0;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Saturday afternoon</p>
      <h1 class="wordmark" style="font-size:clamp(2rem,6vw,3.6rem);">The hunt</h1>
      <p class="tagline">
        You installed a phone system this morning. Here is how the family finds
        out what it does without anybody reading a runbook.
      </p>
      <Divider />
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="panel">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The house phone rings. Dad is calling from the driveway, and his name
          is on the little screen, because he is on the list. &ldquo;Answer it
          in the kitchen,&rdquo; he says, and hangs up. On the kitchen table
          there is an envelope.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>How many chairs are there at the table where this family
          eats?</em> Six. She dials <code>*6</code> and then <code>6</code>, and
          the phone speaks to her in her father&rsquo;s voice: take this phone to
          the theater, call <code>101</code>, and ask for the password. Say
          please.
        </p>
        <p>
          Her brother is in the theater with the other handset. He has the
          password and will not say it until she calls him on the phone, which is
          the rule, and which is the point. Under a seat, another envelope:
          everyone dial <code>600</code>. Three handsets land in the same
          bridge and they say the password together, badly, and laugh.
        </p>
        <p>
          The next clue sends her to call Grandma, who is a name on the screen
          now and who has been briefed. Grandma knows how many stairs there are.
          At the top of the stairs there is an envelope that asks her to count
          them anyway. <code>*6</code>, fourteen. Her father&rsquo;s voice again:
          you have won. Dial <code>500</code> and tell the whole house.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every phone in the house answers itself on speaker at once, and she
          tells the whole house. At dinner there is an envelope under her plate.
          The prize is extra rice.
        </p>

        <h2>What just happened</h2>
        <p>
          Every rung of the verification ladder &mdash; inbound, the allow-list,
          handset to handset, the conference bridge, an outside call, paging
          &mdash; got exercised by a child holding a cordless handset. The
          operator learned the same things a terminal would have told them, and
          the family learned the phone.
        </p>
        <p>
          There is no game engine. The mechanism is four commented-out lines of
          dialplan: <code>*6</code> followed by a number plays the greeting of
          the mailbox with that number. A parent records each clue as a greeting
          from any handset. A wrong answer is a mailbox with no greeting, and the
          phone says so. Progress lives in the envelopes, which is where a
          scavenger hunt keeps it anyway.
        </p>
        <p>
          The kit ships no audio. Every family records its own clues, in its own
          voices, because the clue that sends a child to the theater should sound
          like the person who hid it there.
        </p>

        <h2>One rule</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Children never hold a credential.</strong> The answers are counts
          of things in the house, never a PIN and never a password the phone
          really uses. A player only ever dials <code>*6</code> and a number.
        </p>

        <h2>Run it in your house</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Uncomment the hunt lines in <code>extensions.conf</code> and add a
            mailbox per clue, named for its answer.</li>
          <li>Record each clue as that mailbox&rsquo;s greeting from any handset:
            <code>*98</code>, the mailbox, its PIN, <code>0</code>, <code>1</code>.</li>
          <li>Print the cards, fill in your house&rsquo;s answers, hide the
            envelopes, and call the house.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The full guide is
          <a href="https://github.com/ericdmoore/call-me-maybe/blob/main/docs/HUNT.md">docs/HUNT.md</a>,
          and the printable cards are
          <a href="https://github.com/ericdmoore/call-me-maybe/blob/main/docs/hunt/cards.md">docs/hunt/cards.md</a>
          (CC BY-SA 4.0, archetypes only, blanks for your own).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
